# 🌍 English Contemplation

## **One Jewel — Three Worlds**

The jewel is one.

One person receives it and rejoices.

Another fails to obtain it and becomes angry.

A third sees it without either excitement or resentment.

If joy and anger belonged inherently to the jewel, everyone would experience the same thing.

They do not.

The object may be common.

The psychological world constructed around it is personal.

This distinction is spiritually powerful.

Vedānta is not necessarily asking us first to destroy the object, abandon the world, or suppress experience.

It asks us to notice what the mind adds:

**“I need this.”**

**“This must not happen.”**

**“This proves my worth.”**

**“Without this I cannot be happy.”**

There the common world becomes **my world of bondage**.

The third person in the jewel example reveals another possibility:

**to see without immediately possessing, rejecting, or defining oneself through what is seen.**

The jewel remains.

Seeing remains.

Life continues.

But bondage need not accompany perception.

And behind joy, anger and equanimity alike remains the same luminous fact:

### **they are all known.**

That knowing Consciousness is not enriched when the jewel arrives and is not diminished when the jewel disappears.

Perhaps this is where the teaching on **jīva-sṛṣṭi** quietly meets the Yoga Vāsiṣṭha's teaching on **Chiti**.

The worlds of experience change.

### **The light by which they are known does not.**

